#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130203} :activity/id #uuid "1e370681-4235-581a-8e92-bfa4ed63d765" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050390 :topic/id #uuid "4543eb7d-4646-5b7f-9bb9-14d0a8526a45" :topic/math-academy-id 2060} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130203 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130455 :tutorial/content "When evaluating triple integrals, it's sometimes easier to change the variables from Cartesian coordinates to spherical polar coordinates.\n\nFor example, suppose we want to evaluate the triple integral\n\n$$\\displaystyle \\iiint\\limits_R \\left(x^2 + y^2 + z^2\\right)^{3/2} \\: \\textrm{d}V$$\n\nwhere $R$ is the region enclosed by the sphere of radius $1$ centered at $O,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/08/0809a74f0aca2b3042b7f8db6783f9d6a13bc84bfc75ddcbb4d3076dbf0a2efd.png)\n\nThis integral seems too challenging when written in Cartesian coordinates. However, we should recognize that the problem lends itself to spherical polar coordinates because\n\n- the domain $R$ has a simple spherical polar representation, and\n- the integrand $f(x,y,z)=\\left(x^2+y^2+z^2\\right)^{3/2}$ can also be expressed simply in spherical polar coordinates.\n\nSo, let's transform the entire problem to spherical polar coordinates using the transformation $\\mathbf{T},$ defined by\n\n$$x = \\rho\\cos\\theta\\sin\\phi, \\qquad y = \\rho\\sin\\theta\\sin\\phi, \\qquad z = \\rho\\cos\\phi.$$\n\nThe preimage of $R$ under the action of $\\mathbf{T}$ is the rectangular region $\\Delta,$ given by\n\n$$\\Delta = \\left\\{ (\\rho, \\theta, \\phi) \\, : \\, 0 \\leq \\rho \\leq 1, \\: 0 \\leq \\theta \\lt 2\\pi, \\: 0 \\leq \\phi \\leq \\pi \\right\\},$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/c2/c2ed8a57c5e66687428a524eb148f96672f3a1ace7fbd28d426de065b2fb5f1f.png)\n\nTo express our integral in terms of spherical polar coordinates, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\\iiint\\limits_{R} f(x,y,z) \\, \\mathrm{d}V = \\iiint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\, f(\\rho\\cos\\theta\\sin\\phi, \\rho\\sin\\theta\\sin\\phi, \\rho\\cos\\phi) \\: \\left| \\dfrac{\\partial (x,y,z)}{\\partial (\\rho, \\theta, \\phi)} \\right| \\: \\mathrm{d}\\rho \\, \\mathrm{d}\\theta \\, \\mathrm{d}\\phi.$$\n\nIt can be shown that the absolute value of the Jacobian determinant for a spherical change of variables is\n\n$$\\left| \\dfrac{\\partial (x,y,z)}{\\partial (\\rho, \\theta, \\phi)} \\right| = \\rho^2\\sin\\phi.$$\n\nTherefore, the change of variables formula is\n\n$$\\iiint\\limits_{R} f(x,y,z) \\, \\mathrm{d}V = \\iiint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\, f(\\rho\\cos\\theta\\sin\\phi, \\rho\\sin\\theta\\sin\\phi, \\rho\\cos\\phi) \\: \\rho^2\\sin\\phi \\: \\mathrm{d}\\rho \\, \\mathrm{d}\\theta \\, \\mathrm{d}\\phi.$$\n\nNow, let's write our function $f(x,y,z) = \\left(x^2 + y^2 + z^2\\right)^{3/2}$ in spherical polar coordinates: $$\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =f(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\operatorname{sin}ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}\\theta \\operatorname{sin}ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}ϕ) \\\\ & ={({\\rho }^{2})}^{3/2} \\\\ & ={\\rho }^{3}\\end{aligned}$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain\n$$\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2})}^{3/2}\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}{\\rho }^{3}\\cdot {\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ \\\\ & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\rho }^{5}\\operatorname{sin}ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ \\\\ & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}ϕ\\,dϕ\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }d\\theta \\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\rho }^{5}\\,d\\rho \\\\ & =[-\\operatorname{cos}ϕ]_{ϕ=0}^{ϕ=\\pi }\\cdot [\\theta ]_{\\theta =0}^{\\theta =2\\pi }\\cdot {[\\frac{1}{6}{\\rho }^{6}]}_{\\rho =0}^{\\rho =1}\\,d\\rho \\\\ & =-(-1-1)\\cdot (2\\pi -0)\\cdot \\frac{1}{6}(1-0) \\\\ & =2\\cdot 2\\pi \\cdot \\frac{1}{6} \\\\ & =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "b86207dc-5c0f-5e73-a894-7cd428b18036" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7149 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "0355ca44-2058-5e43-b562-1ba67c747a89" :step/math-academy-id 42751 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130204}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130204 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129264 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128841 :question/id #uuid "0e34fc48-0bfc-57b3-82fd-e5ed205995a9" :question/math-academy-id "e-11372"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f7a75441-d233-5a64-8d87-b3138c2d31be" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048692} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049639}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129523} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129756} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373993} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374013} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374034}] :knowledge-point/title "Rewriting a Triple Integral Defined Over a Sphere"} :step/id #uuid "187ae894-50e1-5dc4-a7bd-e52db3a63568" :step/math-academy-id 42752 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130228}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130205 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129265 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128842 :question/id #uuid "c56b3218-2c96-59fe-a5e3-af6f01164af4" :question/math-academy-id "e-11373"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7b1d5b6a-3e7b-567d-a61f-d18f55924912" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129758} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129759} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374076} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374104} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374111} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374118}] :knowledge-point/title "Rewriting a Triple Integral Defined Over a Shifted Sphere"} :step/id #uuid "6ef297ec-41d5-5d03-8b56-8189aecc9f8e" :step/math-academy-id 42753 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130206}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130206 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129266 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128843 :question/id #uuid "85182b2a-10d4-5663-831b-41623c395cce" :question/math-academy-id "e-11374"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "00ea201b-cf90-54cf-bd20-d7e990345710" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129749} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129761} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371961} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374048} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374055} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374062} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374069}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Triple Integral by Converting to Spherical Polar Coordinates"} :step/id #uuid "6eb5bbde-3ae3-519e-a13a-011708f318d7" :step/math-academy-id 42755 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130235}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130228 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129281 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128859 :question/id #uuid "2adb62b7-c889-5c4c-be0f-8f26a240475c" :question/math-academy-id "e-12409"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3d85b11c-498d-53b7-98f8-b742b46e8dc2" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129524} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129760} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374000} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374020} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374027} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374041}] :knowledge-point/title "Rewriting a Triple Integral Defined Over a Part-Sphere"} :step/id #uuid "fb036686-92a4-5d96-ae09-318717eb493f" :step/math-academy-id 44584 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130205}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130235 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130468 :tutorial/content "Throughout this lesson, we've used the fact that $$\\left| \\dfrac{\\partial (x, y, z)}{\\partial (\\rho, \\theta, \\phi)} \\right| = \\rho^2 \\sin{\\phi}.$$\n\nLet's now prove this result.\n\nGiven the spherical coordinate transformations\n\n$$x = \\rho\\cos\\theta\\sin\\phi, \\qquad y = \\rho\\sin\\theta\\sin\\phi, \\qquad z = \\rho\\cos\\phi,$$\n\nwe first calculate the required partial derivatives:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂\\rho } & =\\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\operatorname{sin}ϕ & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂\\theta } & =-\\rho \\operatorname{sin}\\theta \\operatorname{sin}ϕ & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂ϕ} & =\\rho \\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\operatorname{cos}ϕ \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂\\rho } & =\\operatorname{sin}\\theta \\operatorname{sin}ϕ & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂\\theta } & =\\rho \\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\operatorname{sin}ϕ & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂ϕ} & =\\rho \\operatorname{sin}\\theta \\operatorname{cos}ϕ \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂\\rho } & =\\operatorname{cos}ϕ & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂\\theta } & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂ϕ} & =-\\rho \\operatorname{sin}ϕ\\end{aligned}$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\operatorname{sin}ϕ & -\\rho \\operatorname{sin}\\theta \\operatorname{sin}ϕ & \\rho \\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\operatorname{cos}ϕ \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}\\theta \\operatorname{sin}ϕ & \\rho \\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\operatorname{sin}ϕ & \\rho \\operatorname{sin}\\theta \\operatorname{cos}ϕ \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}ϕ & 0 & -\\rho \\operatorname{sin}ϕ\\end{aligned}| \\\\ & =\\operatorname{cos}ϕ|\\begin{aligned}-\\rho \\operatorname{sin}\\theta \\operatorname{sin}ϕ & \\rho \\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\operatorname{cos}ϕ \\\\ \\rho \\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\operatorname{sin}ϕ & \\rho \\operatorname{sin}\\theta \\operatorname{cos}ϕ\\end{aligned}|-\\rho \\operatorname{sin}ϕ|\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\operatorname{sin}ϕ & -\\rho \\operatorname{sin}\\theta \\operatorname{sin}ϕ \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}\\theta \\operatorname{sin}ϕ & \\rho \\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\operatorname{sin}ϕ\\end{aligned}| \\\\ & =\\operatorname{cos}ϕ\\cdot (-{\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}ϕ\\operatorname{cos}ϕ({sin}^{2}\\theta +{cos}^{2}\\theta ))-\\rho \\operatorname{sin}ϕ\\cdot \\rho {sin}^{2}ϕ\\cdot ({cos}^{2}\\theta +{sin}^{2}\\theta ) \\\\ & =-{\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}ϕ{cos}^{2}ϕ-{\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}ϕ{sin}^{2}ϕ \\\\ & =-{\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}ϕ({cos}^{2}ϕ+{sin}^{2}ϕ) \\\\ & =-{\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}ϕ.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that $$\\left| \\dfrac{\\partial (x, y, z)}{\\partial (\\rho, \\theta, \\phi)} \\right| = \\rho^2 \\sin{\\phi}.$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "86bd6cae-b4e6-5da9-80b3-b884c320bda3" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7720 :tutorial/title "Deriving the Jacobian for Spherical Polar Coordinates"} :step/id #uuid "96fb4483-0ca7-52c1-9f83-46c894da5471" :step/math-academy-id 44767 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130236}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130236 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130469 :tutorial/content "We can also derive the change of variables formula for spherical polar coordinates using geometry.\n\nConsider a bounded spherical box $R,$ given by\n\n$$R = \\big\\{ (\\rho,\\theta,\\phi) \\: | \\: a \\leq \\rho \\leq b, \\:\\: \\alpha \\leq \\theta \\leq \\beta, \\:\\: \\gamma \\leq \\phi \\leq \\psi \\big\\}.$$\n\nWe can divide each interval into $l,m,$ and $n$ subdivisions such that\n\n$$\\Delta\\rho = \\dfrac{b-a}{l},\\qquad \\Delta\\theta = \\dfrac{\\beta-\\alpha}{m},\\qquad \\Delta\\phi = \\dfrac{\\psi-\\gamma}{n}.$$\n\nThis splits the region into \"spherical wedges,\" as shown in the following diagram.\n\n![](images/88/88be4c6a09062321d20ac7f48dfbb6be796cdd6b5bb1b7f6adc49a6ae1da609b.png)\n\nZooming in on one of these wedges, we obtain a shape resembling a rectangular solid.\n\n![](images/5e/5e5f7acb4d057e856c66e9d5626f81178ca26c0b7ce83cdf7290b1f4c64b1852.png)\n\nLet's note the volume of this solid as $\\Delta V.$ To approximate the value of $\\Delta V,$ we use the formula for the volume of a rectangular solid:\n\n$$\\Delta V \\approx lwh$$\n\nClearly, the height of the rectangular solid is $h = \\Delta \\rho.$ We can find the remaining dimensions using simple geometry.\n\nFirst, we need to find the length of the radius $r$ in the $xy$-plane. Using the following triangle and a side-on view of the wedge, we conclude that $r=\\rho\\sin\\phi.$\n\n![](images/01/0122c6c3b813bbe2821dbede794b6027cd69002dd1828925811211045a0d67e8.png)\n\nLet's add this to our diagram.\n\n![](images/d4/d41b538e9bb3add9bae1e3a24c9f87ae282a8b969f0c0dc4acd374ad777de74a.png)\n\nFinally, we can use the arc length formula $s = r \\cdot \\theta$ to find the length $l$ and width $w.$\n\n![](images/88/882b9243d1189c098aaf6e1e45ca7ec97d480b1f2c3a8fe23d5e39a6c2313ed2.png)\n\nThe length of our wedge is $$l = \\rho \\cdot \\Delta\\phi = \\rho\\Delta\\phi$$ and the width is $$w = \\rho\\sin\\phi \\cdot \\Delta\\theta = \\rho\\sin\\phi\\Delta\\theta.$$\n\nCalculating the volume as a rectangular solid, we obtain\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}ΔV & \\approx lwh \\\\ & =(\\rho Δϕ)\\cdot (\\rho \\operatorname{sin}ϕΔ\\theta )\\cdot (Δ\\rho ) \\\\ & ={\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}ϕ\\,Δϕ\\,Δ\\theta \\,Δ\\rho .\\end{aligned}$$\n\nAs a result, we define the triple integral in spherical coordinates as the limit of a triple Riemann sum, where $(\\rho_{ijk},\\theta_{ijk},\\phi_{ijk})$ being any sample point in the spherical subregion $B{ijk}{:}$\n\n$$\\lim\\limits_{l,m,n \\to \\infty} \\sum\\limits_{i=1}^{l} \\sum\\limits_{j=1}^{m} \\sum\\limits_{k=1}^{n} f(\\rho_{ijk},\\theta_{ijk},\\phi_{ijk}) (\\rho_{ijk})^2 \\sin \\phi_{ijk} \\:\\Delta\\rho\\:\\Delta\\theta\\:\\Delta\\phi$$\n\nAs we increase the number of subregions, the size of $\\Delta\\rho,$ $\\Delta\\theta,$ and $\\Delta\\phi$ all goes to zero, and the volume of $R$ is more accurately approximated, giving\n\n$$\\textrm{d}V = \\rho^2 \\sin\\phi \\:\\textrm{d}\\rho\\:\\textrm{d}\\theta\\:\\textrm{d}\\phi.$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that if $f(\\rho,\\theta,\\phi)$ is continuous on a spherical box $R = [a,b] \\times [\\alpha,\\beta] \\times [\\gamma, \\psi],$ then $$\\iiint\\limits_{R} f(\\rho,\\theta,\\phi) \\:\\textrm{d}V = \\iiint\\limits_{R} f(\\rho,\\theta,\\phi) \\rho^2 \\sin\\phi \\:\\textrm{d}\\rho\\:\\textrm{d}\\theta\\:\\textrm{d}\\phi = \\int_{\\gamma}^{\\psi}\\int_{\\alpha}^{\\beta}\\int_{a}^{b} f(\\rho,\\theta,\\phi) \\rho^2 \\sin\\phi \\:\\textrm{d}\\rho\\:\\textrm{d}\\theta\\:\\textrm{d}\\phi.$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "444d88e7-62f3-5333-98ad-9ee9e22d6a46" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7730 :tutorial/title "Deriving the Change of Variables Formula Using Geometry"} :step/id #uuid "68b6f2fa-a8c8-5b52-98b8-fd02b1454611" :step/math-academy-id 44789}] :activity/title "Triple Integrals in Spherical Polar Coordinates" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186129390}]}
